## POM - PageObjectModel ##
-> The primary goal of the Page Object Model (POM) is to hide the heavy lifting of WebDriver and expose only the intent of the user.

-> Tests should express bissnes behavior; Page Objects should express mechanics.

-> The test should not know how the site works. The Page Objects should know the site's UI structure, locators, actions, and navigation.

-> URLs and site-specific navigation logic should usually live outside the test, in your POM/page layer.

1. Hide driver.get() inside the Page Object
Your test shouldn't be responsible for navigating the driver. The LoginPage should know how to open itself.

Standard: Create an open() or load() method inside your LoginPage class.

2. Group Actions into High-Level Business Flows
Instead of calling enter_username, enter_password, and click_login individually in every test, group them into a single method inside the POM.

Standard: Create a login(username, password) method on the LoginPage.

3. Return the Next Page Object (Fluent Interface)
When a user clicks "login", the application transitions to a new page. Your POM should mirror this by returning the Page Object of the page you land on.

Standard: click_login() (or your grouped login() method) should return a ClubManagementPage or UserSettingsDropdown object upon success, or return self (the LoginPage) if the login fails.

4. Hide "Waits" Inside the Page Object
Your test file has login_page.wait_for_url_contain(...). Tests should ideally contain no wait logic.

Standard: The synchronization (waiting for the URL to change) should happen automatically inside the login() or logout_click() methods before returning the next page object.

5. Remove Hardcoded Strings & Redundant Assertions
assert ... is True is unpythonic. Use assert ....

Hardcoded strings like "Nieprawidłowy login lub hasło." should be stored as constants in the LoginPage class or a localization file, so if the Polish translation changes, you only update it in one place.